\title{OPT-B: the loader thread's CPU - what it is made of in the executable, and four changes to the reader, the loader and the block writer that leave the store word for word as it was}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{loader}
\tag{store}
\tag{performance}
\tag{platform}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Optimisation wave after the first release, agent "OPT-B", 2026-11-25 (05:20 to 09:07 UTC). The lever is the second of [the profile of the final default configuration](rep-P11), [the loader thread](ins-DBTJ): one thread that reads the export, links, builds the store and packs the proof blocks, 11.46 s of CPU per Mathlib pass in the executable and 8.60 s on the JVM launcher there. The task: take CPU out of that thread in \code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-load} and \code{rlvm-store} without changing the store it builds. Nothing in the repository was changed by this work: the result is three patches, measured and gated on a private copy of the tracked sources. \code{rlvm-machine} and \code{rlvm-level} were not touched.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\table{
\tr{\th{base -> candidate, interleaved rounds on a busy host (section 1)}\th{Init}\th{cslib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{the executable: instructions of one load, G}\td{7.62 -> 6.02}\td{53.8 -> 43.8}\td{not counted}\td{136.0 -> 109.1}}
\tr{\td{the executable: the load alone, s}\td{0.584 -> 0.478 (-18 \%)}\td{4.48 -> 3.92 (-13 \%)}\td{not measured}\td{11.08 -> 9.81 (-11 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the executable: CPU of the loader thread in a pass, s}\td{not measured}\td{4.95 -> 4.45}\td{2.62 -> 2.15}\td{12.12 -> 10.62}}
\tr{\td{the executable: warm pass, s (the guard)}\td{0.725 -> 0.677 (-6.6 \%)}\td{5.693 -> 5.288 (-7.1 \%)}\td{3.920 -> 3.751 (-4.3 \%)}\td{14.532 -> 14.160 (-2.6 \%)}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher: warm pass, s (the guard)}\td{0.568 -> 0.544 (-4.2 \%)}\td{4.179 -> 4.055 (-3.0 \%)}\td{3.136 -> 3.045 (-2.9 \%)}\td{11.859 -> 11.361 (-4.2 \%)}}
}

\ol{
\li{\strong{What the thread is made of} (section 2). In the executable a Mathlib pass costs the loader thread 147 G instructions at 3.1 instructions per cycle, 1,310 instructions per line of the export. A fifth of them were not work but the way the ahead-of-time compiler had laid the work out: the scanner's word load and its number conversion were calls (22 and 65 instructions around one load and one conversion, six calls per \code{app} line, 23 \% of all instructions of the thread), and a proof-private record went through four calls and about 410 instructions on its way from the segment into its block. The rest is memory: the location table, promotion, the name table and the children of shared nodes take two thirds of the thread's TLB walks, and the kernel takes 12.9 \% of its time for 0.85 million page faults per pass.}
\li{\strong{Why the executable's loader is dearer than the JVM's} (section 3). Three mechanisms, each visible in the machine code: a small method that the compiler does not inline costs a stack check, a frame and a safepoint poll, 14 instructions, and without a profile it is not inlined into a large loop; a call anywhere inside a loop, also one that is never made, makes the compiler keep the loop's values in the stack frame; and branches on the data (how many digits, which kind of reference, how many bytes) are mispredicted 2.8 times per record. The JVM's compiler removes the first two with its profile. None of this is specific to the loader: it is the disease [the workers have](ins-TVGI).}
\li{\strong{What was changed} (section 4): the scanner converts \code{app} lines, six lines of seven, in a loop of its own with the loads and the conversion written in place, no call and no branch on the number of digits; the reader hands such lines over as a stretch and the loader appends a stretch to its segment in one loop; the sweep hands the proof-private records of a segment to the block writer as a stretch, with the references converted without a branch; the block writer writes the common records of a stretch in a loop without a call and stores a reference as one word. Each stretch has a record-by-record default that defines what it must do, and each loop falls back to the old path for whatever is not the common case.}
\li{\strong{What it gives} (section 5): the load takes a fifth fewer instructions on every corpus counted and 11 to 18 \% less time alone; in a Mathlib pass the loader thread needs 1.5 s less CPU (12.12 to 10.62 s). The guard holds on both launchers over three interleaved rounds: in the executable the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 2.170 to 2.082 s (-4.1 \%), seven corpora are 1.4 to 7.1 \% faster and reachability is at +0.1 \%; Mathlib goes from 14.53 to 14.16 s, 0.37 s, where the profile's estimate was 0.25 to 0.4 s. On the JVM launcher the geometric mean goes down by 3.8 \% and every corpus is faster.}
\li{\strong{The store is the same store} (section 6): the differential digest of everything a load builds is identical to the base's in 25 reader configurations on 17,158 small files, the 263 arena tests below 100 MB, 847 prelude mutants and the eight corpora, under both policies; tick counts, record counts and declines of every corpus are identical with one worker.}
\li{\strong{Verdict} (section 7): \strong{GO} for the default configuration: a cost-only change with the store word for word the same, every gate passed with the release's counts, and the guard held on the executable and on the JVM launcher.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Base.} A private copy of the tracked sources of the main tree, taken at 05:23 UTC, built as a whole by \src{scripts/build.sh} into a private development distribution: the executable (GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 native image, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, the heap settings and the inline lists of the release) and the JVM launcher (HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 with C2, the launcher's flags, heap of the machine \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). Jars sha256 prefix \code{\verb~|026a65358fe0~}. Its one-thread store digests equal the reference digests that [the review of the reference engine's caches](rep-reference-caches) used. When the work ended the three modules of the main tree were still the base's, file for file.}
\li{\strong{Candidate.} The same copy with the three patches of section 8, built the same way: \code{rlvm-export} \code{\verb~|38c922c4fccd~}, \code{rlvm-load} \code{\verb~|a2a091b291fa~}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{\verb~|a93a52584ee7~}; every other jar is built from the base's source.}
\li{\strong{Symbolised images.} A second image of each with \code{\verb~|-g~} (the same code; DWARF with inlined frames) for \code{perf}, in the arena container. No guard time is from them.}
\li{\strong{Engine and run line.} A run without \code{\verb~|-X engine~}: the machine with its default switch set, policy practical, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, one scanner step on the loader thread: \code{\verb~|rlvm[-jvm] --threads 8 --stats -X passes=N FILE~}. "The load alone" is the same executable with \code{\verb~|-X stop-after=load~}: the reader, the loader and the store on one thread and nothing else, three loads in a process.}
\li{\strong{Instruments.} Wall clock and CPU from the pass lines; CPU per thread from the kernel's per-thread counters, polled every 100 ms (the poller of rep-P11); shares of the JVM's loader thread from \code{\verb~|jdk.CPUTimeSample~} (2 ms of CPU per sample, attached after pass 3 of 6); shares of the image's loader thread from \code{\verb~|perf record~} in the arena container (\code{\verb~|cpu-clock~} every 0.5 ms, cycles, instructions, last-level read misses, branch mispredictions, data TLB walks, cycles stalled on memory; attached after pass 2 of 4), every sample address resolved to its chain of inlined frames; instructions, cycles and mispredicted branches of whole processes from \code{\verb~|perf stat~} in the same container (counts, not times: they were not taken in quiet windows).}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB. Every time in seconds was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores, heavy jobs behind \src{bench/slot.sh}. \strong{The host was not quiet}: the two other agents of the wave built, gated and measured during the whole work (load average 37 to 42), and the measurement cores share memory bandwidth and the last-level cache with the other 12. The base's Mathlib pass takes 15.5 to 16.6 s in these windows, not the 14.46 s of rep-P11. Only numbers of interleaved rounds inside one window are compared.}
\li{\strong{Corpora.} Init, Std, cedar, reachability, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib, Mathlib: the exports of the benchmark tables.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The loader thread of the base, itemised}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 Level 1}

\table{
\tr{\th{Mathlib, pipelined pass, 8 workers (base)}\th{JVM launcher, passes 2 to 6}\th{executable, passes 1 to 4 (symbolised image, container)}}
\tr{\td{pass, s}\td{11.74 to 12.59}\td{14.84 to 16.64}}
\tr{\td{loader thread, CPU s}\td{9.16 to 9.42}\td{11.63 to 12.47}}
\tr{\td{- of it kernel, s}\td{0.30 to 0.34}\td{1.11 to 1.16}}
\tr{\td{minor page faults of the loader thread}\td{95,300 to 95,600}\td{846,000 to 867,000}}
\tr{\td{workers, CPU s}\td{76.8 to 80.9}\td{99.9 to 108.0}}
}

\p{The executable's loader needs 1.25 to 1.34 times the JVM's CPU, as in rep-P11 (1.33). 0.8 s of the difference is kernel time for 0.76 million more page faults. The JVM's 95,000 faults are as many as the mapped file has steps of 64 KiB; the image adds about one fault per 4 KiB page of every array the load allocates, 3.0 GB per pass, because its collector gives large arrays back to the system and gets fresh pages for the next ones. (The kernel's own symbols are not readable in the container; one kernel address has a fifth of the kernel samples, 0.32 s per pass, which is what clearing 3 GB costs.)}
}

