\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Mem.u64~}, called}\td{5.7 \%}\td{8.6 \%}\td{22 per 8-byte load}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ExportReader.sequential~} with \code{\verb~|Scanner.scan~} inlined}\td{5.5 \%}\td{8.4 \%}\td{110 per line}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.WriterImpl.app~}}\td{5.4 \%}\td{8.9 \%}\td{167 per block record}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.sweep~}}\td{5.4 \%}\td{8.5 \%}\td{117 per record}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.resolvePending~}}\td{5.2 \%}\td{2.8 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NodeCtx.writeApp~}}\td{4.4 \%}\td{3.3 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.nameStr~} with the name table inlined}\td{4.0 \%}\td{1.4 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.promote~}, \code{parse}, \code{seek}, \code{pending}, \code{rd}}\td{8.7 \%}\td{3.5 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|TermStore.touch~}, \code{appendExport}, \code{ref}}\td{6.3 \%}\td{6.3 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NameTable.internNum~}}\td{3.4 \%}\td{1.0 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{copy of the mapped file}\td{2.4 \%}\td{1.9 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.ref~}, called twice per block record}\td{2.1 \%}\td{3.4 \%}\td{32 per call}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Loader.exprApp~}}\td{1.8 \%}\td{3.0 \%}\td{41 per \code{app} record}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Linker.app~}}\td{1.5 \%}\td{2.4 \%}\td{33 per \code{app} record}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The scanner is 22 \% of the time and 31 \% of the instructions}: 420 instructions for a line of 48 bytes, of which 196 are three calls of the number conversion and 112 are five calls of a one-line method that loads eight bytes.}
\li{\strong{A proof-private record costs about 410 instructions between the segment and its block}: the sweep's dispatch, two calls that turn a child into a block reference, the call of the writer, in it the bookkeeping of the record index and two references, each with a call for a shared term. Four fifths of the expression records are proof-private.}
\li{\strong{The memory-bound items are the same on both platforms}: the read of the location of an earlier record (20 \% of the thread's TLB walks, 13 \% of its last-level misses), promotion (22 \% and 20 \%), the name table (16 \% and 9 \%), the children of a shared node (17 \% and 10 \%). They were left alone.}
\li{\strong{The kernel} has more than a fifth of the thread's last-level misses.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Why the executable's loader is dearer: three things the ahead-of-time compiler does}

\ol{
\li{\strong{A small method that is not inlined is not small.} \code{\verb~|Mem.u64~} is one bounds-checked load. As a function of the image it is a stack-overflow check (4 instructions), a frame (3), the null and bounds checks (7), the load (3), the frame's end (2) and a safepoint poll before the return (3): 22 instructions. The compiler inlines it into the small \code{\verb~|Scanner.num~} and not into the scan loop, which it had already filled by inlining \code{\verb~|Scanner.scan~} into its caller. Without a profile it cannot know that this loop is where the thread lives. The build of rep-P11 with four times the inliner's budgets did not move the pass; a force-inline list for 25 methods of the reader and the loader, tried in this work, gave -9 \% on the load of Init and -1 to -6 \% on the load of Mathlib in three rounds.}
\li{\strong{A call inside a loop costs the loop its registers.} The first version of the block writer's stretch loop had everything in locals and two calls on rare paths (a new chunk of the record index, a reference to a node of the open unit). The image's code for it kept the loop's values in the stack frame and reloaded them in every iteration: about 195 instructions per record, more than the calls it replaced. The image's calling convention has no register that survives a call, so every value that lives across a possible call needs a place in the frame, and the allocator then reloads it inside the loop. The same loop with the rare cases moved behind a \code{break}, out of the loop, is straight-line code.}
\li{\strong{Branches on the data.} The base mispredicts 116 million branches per load of cslib, 2.8 per expression record: the number of digits of a number, the kind of a child (an earlier shared term, a shared record of the segment, a block record), the number of bytes of a reference. An intermediate candidate with fewer instructions mispredicted 141 million. Written as arithmetic - the number of digits as a shift count from the trailing zeros, a child's kind as a mask from the sign, the bytes of a reference as one word with a length from the leading zeros - they cost a few instructions more and are never wrong: 69 million in the candidate.}
}

\p{The first two are what a profile gives the JVM's compiler for nothing; the third costs both platforms. They are the image-specific slow paths the lever asked about, and they are properties of how hot code is written for this compiler, not of the loader: see [the insight](ins-BGII).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The changes}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Scanner} (\code{rlvm-export}). Word loads are written through the handle where they stand, not through the one-line method. \code{app} lines have a loop of their own, \code{\verb~|Scanner.appStretch~}: it takes consecutive lines that are exactly the template, converts their three numbers in place (one to nine digits, no branch on the number of digits, no call), and stops in front of the first line that is anything else; that line goes through the code that was there before, including the general path. A line is taken by the loop only if the old test of the template takes it, with the same numbers.}
\li{\strong{Stretches of app records} (\code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-load}). The scanner hands the lines of a stretch over together (\code{\verb~|RecordOut.apps~}; the default, which the tapes of the scanner threads use, makes the single calls). The linker offers the longest prefix that its fast path would take - dense ids with a known delta, the next id, both children earlier expressions - to the sink (\code{\verb~|ExportSink.exprApps~}, default: refuse) and makes the single calls for a sink that refuses and for every record that is not of that kind, so an error has its record and its line. The loader takes a stretch in one loop that does what \code{exprApp} does, and refuses whenever the record-by-record path has something else to do: metadata was seen, referrer counting, the segment limit, a child that is not an earlier record. Nothing is counted before the whole stretch is taken.}
\li{\strong{The sweep} (\code{rlvm-load}). A stretch of consecutive proof-private APP, LAM, PI and LET records has its children turned into block references in place - the segment is consumed by the sweep - and its locations set by a leaf loop without a branch on the kind of a child, and goes to the target in one call (\code{\verb~|LoadTarget.blockRecords~}, default: the single calls). Between the first and the last record of a stretch nothing is written to the shared region, so the order of everything that sets a sharing flag is the order of the single calls. The loop over the references to earlier records is split the same way: the references to shared records in a loop without a call, a promotion or a packed run outside it, in the same order.}
\li{\strong{The block writer} (\code{rlvm-store}). \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.Writer.records~} is the contract (default: the single calls \code{app}, \code{lam}, \code{pi}, \code{let}); the writer's own loop writes the common records - APP, LAM and PI whose references are earlier records of the block or shared terms of an earlier unit below the first unresolved node, for which a reference has no effect on the shared region - without a call, and every other record (a LET, a reference to a node of the open unit or to an unresolved one, a wrong reference) with the checks of the single calls. The bytes, the record index for promotion and the block row are those of the single calls.}
}

\p{Why this is safe: no rule of the reader, the loader or the store changed. Every new path has the old path as its definition - an interface default that makes the single calls - and takes only the cases for which the two are the same by construction; the reasons stand in the comments of the methods. The evidence is section 6. What the change is not: it does not touch how records are split into shared nodes and blocks, when a record is promoted, how a unit is published, or anything a worker reads. It is cost only: the engines see the same store and count the same ticks.}
}

